  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-1)=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of ${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{1}{6}.$",
